Python can write `return lo, hi` and unpack the pair. Java has no tuple type and no unpacking, and records (named data carriers) only arrive in Java 16. When a method must hand back two values of the same type, the simplest fix is a small array:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int[] temps = {18, 25, 9, 31, 22};
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(minMax(temps)));
    }

    {{code}}
}
```

```java
static int[] minMax(int[] a) {
    int min = a[0];
    int max = a[0];
    for (int x : a) {
        min = Math.min(min, x);
        max = Math.max(max, x);
    }
    return new int[]{min, max};
}
```

`new int[]{min, max}` builds the array right in the `return`. You need the `new int[]` part: the short `{min, max}` form works only when declaring an array variable, so `return {min, max};` does not compile. The caller stores the result once and reads each part by position:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int[] temps = {18, 25, 9, 31, 22};
        {{code}}
        System.out.println("Low: " + low);
        System.out.println("High: " + high);
    }

    static int[] minMax(int[] a) {
        int min = a[0];
        int max = a[0];
        for (int x : a) {
            min = Math.min(min, x);
            max = Math.max(max, x);
        }
        return new int[]{min, max};
    }
}
```

```java
int[] result = minMax(temps);
int low = result[0];
int high = result[1];
```

Gotcha: positions have no names, so nothing stops you from mixing up `result[0]` and `result[1]`. Keep the order obvious from the method name and state it in a comment. Call the method once and keep the array, instead of calling it twice to get each part. This trick only works when both values share a type. For a name and a number, use a `Map.Entry` pair instead, as the next part shows.

example.code:
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] temps = {18, 25, 9, 31, 22};
        int[] result = minMax(temps);
        System.out.println("Min: " + result[0]);
        System.out.println("Max: " + result[1]);

        int[] scores = {70, 85, 64};
        int[] range = minMax(scores);
        System.out.println("Scores " + Arrays.toString(range) + ", spread " + (range[1] - range[0]));
    }

    // Returns {min, max} of a non-empty array.
    static int[] minMax(int[] a) {
        int min = a[0];
        int max = a[0];
        for (int x : a) {
            if (x < min) {
                min = x;
            }
            if (x > max) {
                max = x;
            }
        }
        return new int[]{min, max};
    }
}

