steps.body:
A list of pairs of ints, such as grid points or moves, is a `List<int[]>`. `List<int>` is not allowed, but `int[]` is an object, so it works as the element type. Add each pair as a fresh two-slot array and read it back by position:

```java
List<int[]> points = new ArrayList<>();
points.add(new int[]{3, 4});
for (int[] p : points) {
    System.out.println("(" + p[0] + ", " + p[1] + ")");
}
```

Print one pair with `p[0]` and `p[1]` or with `Arrays.toString(p)`. Never print the whole list: `println(points)` shows a hash like `[I@6d06d69c` for every pair, because arrays do not print their contents.

The bigger gotcha is equality. Arrays compare by reference, so `points.contains(new int[]{3, 4})` is `false` and `indexOf` returns `-1` even when an equal pair is in the list. A `HashSet<int[]>` has the same problem and never finds a duplicate pair. To search, loop and compare with `Arrays.equals(p, target)`, which checks the contents.

example.code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<int[]> points = new ArrayList<>();
        points.add(new int[]{3, 4});
        points.add(new int[]{-2, 5});
        points.add(new int[]{0, -7});
        points.add(new int[]{1, 2});

        for (int[] p : points) {
            int distance = Math.abs(p[0]) + Math.abs(p[1]);
            System.out.println("(" + p[0] + ", " + p[1] + ") distance " + distance);
        }

        System.out.println("contains: " + points.contains(new int[]{1, 2}));
        System.out.println("indexOf: " + points.indexOf(new int[]{1, 2}));

        int[] target = {1, 2};
        int foundAt = -1;
        for (int i = 0; i < points.size(); i++) {
            if (Arrays.equals(points.get(i), target)) {
                foundAt = i;
                break;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Arrays.equals finds " + Arrays.toString(target) + " at index " + foundAt);
    }
}

